---
// The homepage (docs/plans/website.md §5). Every number here comes from DESIGN.md and
// docs/model-bench.md, with the same caveats: one Mac, one speaker. Change them there first.
import Pill from "../components/Pill.astro";
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base description="EasyVox is a free, open source voice-to-text app for macOS. Hold a shortcut and speak; when you let go, the text is pasted where your cursor is. The speech model runs on your Mac.">
  <main id="main" class="landing">
    <p class="notice">
      <strong>Early development.</strong> EasyVox isn't usable yet, and there's nothing to install. You can
      <a href="/docs/building">build it from source</a>.
    </p>

    <section class="hero">
      <div>
        <h1>EasyVox</h1>
        <p class="tagline">A free, open source voice-to-text app for macOS.</p>
        <p class="lede">
          Hold a shortcut and speak. EasyVox shows the words as it hears them, and when you let go it pastes the
          text where your cursor is.
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <span class="button button-soon">Download: coming soon</span>
          <a class="button button-primary" href="/docs/building">Build from source</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <Pill />
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="private">
      <h2 id="private">Private</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>The speech model runs on your Mac. No audio or text leaves it.</li>
        <li>
          The only time EasyVox goes online is to download a model, which it checks against its sha256 before
          using it.
        </li>
        <li>History keeps the text of each dictation, never the audio.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="light">
      <h2 id="light">Light</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Idle, it does nothing: it wakes about once every 3 seconds and uses no measurable CPU.</li>
        <li>
          While you dictate, it adds about 0.65 W to what the Mac draws. Drawing the overlay's animations on
          screen adds roughly 0.15–0.2 W more.
        </li>
        <li>
          The model is freed after 10 minutes without use, which takes EasyVox from about 670 MB of memory to
          about 90 MB. Your next dictation still starts recording at once.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="caveat">
        Measured in October 2026 on one Mac (an M4 Mac mini), with the default model and one speaker's voice.
        The methods and every number are in the <a href="/docs/model-bench">model bench</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="how">
      <h2 id="how">How it works</h2>
      <ol class="cards">
        <li class="card">
          <h3><span class="step">1</span>Hold <kbd>⌥ Space</kbd></h3>
          <p>Hold the shortcut and speak. You can pick another shortcut, or hold the right Option key on its own.</p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3><span class="step">2</span>Watch the words</h3>
          <p>
            A small pill near the bottom of the screen shows what EasyVox hears. Fainter words are provisional and
            may still change; the rest are confirmed.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3><span class="step">3</span>Let go</h3>
          <p>EasyVox finishes the text and pastes it where your cursor is. It stays on the clipboard too.</p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="models">
      <h2 id="models">Models</h2>
      <p>
        You choose a model in the app, and EasyVox downloads it from Hugging Face. Each one is credited with its
        license.
      </p>
      <ul class="cards">
        <li class="card">
          <h3>Parakeet TDT 0.6B v2</h3>
          <p>The default: the best all-round model. A 539 MB download.</p>
          <p>
            By NVIDIA, under CC BY 4.0.
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v2">Model card</a>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li class="card">
          <h3>Whisper base.en</h3>
          <p>The smallest: for an 8 GB Mac, or when memory matters more than the last bit of accuracy. 85 MB.</p>
          <p>
            By OpenAI, under Apache 2.0.
            <a href="https://huggingface.co/openai/whisper-base.en">Model card</a>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="tinkerers">
      <h2 id="tinkerers">For tinkerers</h2>
      <p>
        Every setting is in one TOML file, and an edit applies as soon as you save it, with no restart. The
        Settings window writes the same file, and leaves your comments alone.
        <a href="/docs/settings">Every key, with its default and what it trades off</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="requirements">
      <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A Mac with Apple silicon</li>
        <li>macOS 26 or later</li>
        <li>English, for now</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="install">
      <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A DMG from the GitHub releases: coming soon.</li>
        <li>A Homebrew cask: coming soon.</li>
        <li><a href="/docs/building">Build from source</a>: the only way for now. You need Xcode, cmake and Rust.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="open-source">
      <h2 id="open-source">Open source</h2>
      <p>
        EasyVox is MIT licensed, and its code will be public on GitHub by the first release. It runs its models on
        <a href="https://github.com/handy-computer/transcribe.cpp">transcribe.cpp</a>, from the team behind
        <a href="https://github.com/cjpais/Handy">Handy</a>, another open source dictation app. The same team makes
        the GGUF builds of both models.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</Base>
